{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1594776539/11/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a6953f00\" lines=\"45-63\">\n<description>\nExtract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops\n  touching the top edge and a grid corner.\n- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.\n- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.\n\nRule\n- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to\n  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.\n- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of\n  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information\n  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a47bf94d\" lines=\"178-189\">\n<description>\nTransmit colored tokens along wire networks, converting each to its complementary 3×3 pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Non-bg cells form wire networks and 3×3 tokens (solid blocks or X/diagonal patterns) near wire endpoints.\n\nRule:\n- Every solid 3×3 token is replaced in-place by a diamond (center + 4 cardinal neighbors).\n- Each token near a wire endpoint sends its complement to the opposite endpoint: solid emits X, X emits diamond.\n\nKey insight: Token shape cycles solid → X → diamond; wires route each complement to the far end.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"465b7d93\" lines=\"97-117\">\n<description>\nMove the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular\n  one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it.\n- The frame interior is initially background.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite from its outside location.\n- Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to\n  interior extremes.\n- Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of\n  adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside\n  those drawn segments.\n\nKey insight:\nThe outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior,\nnot a bitmap to scale by block replication.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d255d7a7\" lines=\"99-114\">\n<description>\nMove the patterned edge band to the opposite edge along every row or column whose band slice contains the trail color.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- The two foreground colors are a frequent trail color and a rarer marker color.\n- All marker pixels lie nearest to one grid edge, and that edge contains trail-color runs that define the source-band thickness.\n\nRule\n- Choose the source edge identified by the marker pixels.\n- A row or column perpendicular to that edge moves if its source-band slice contains the trail color.\n- Copy that slice unchanged to the opposite edge, erase the remaining trail cells to background, and keep marker-only lines fixed.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color identifies the active edge, while the trail color identifies which parallel rows or columns carry an edge-band slice across the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7c66cb00\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDock two-tone sprites into color-matched horizontal bars with a color swap.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Top area: small two-tone sprites (outer color + inner color).\n- Below: horizontal bars spanning most of the row, each with a fill color and border color on edges.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the bar whose fill color equals the sprite's outer color.\n- Place the sprite at its original x-position, aligned to the bar's bottom.\n- Recolor: sprite outer → bar border, sprite inner → bar fill.\n- Erase original sprites from the top area.\n\nKey insight: Color matching is inverted — the sprite's dominant color identifies its target bar, then gets swapped to the bar's accent color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"898e7135\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nInflate a frame's hole-layout and fill each hole with its matching piece.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest component is a monochrome frame enclosing bg holes.\n- Outside pieces composed of k×k blocks; k = side of smallest solid square piece.\n\nRule:\n- Extract connected hole regions inside the frame as slots.\n- Match each piece to a slot by comparing block-level shapes under rotation/flip.\n- Output the layout scaled by k, with pieces placed in slots; frame color as background.\n\nKey insight: Pieces are k×k-block-scaled versions of holes; downsampling reveals shape matches under all 8 orientations.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"37ce87bb\" lines=\"33-47\">\n<description>\nCompute a grey result bar by adding teal bar heights and subtracting red bar heights.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background and several bottom-aligned one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Teal bars are positive operands; red bars are negative operands.\n- The penultimate column is an empty result slot.\n\nRule\n- Keep every input bar unchanged.\n- Compute result height = total teal bar height - total red bar height.\n- Paint that many bottom cells of the penultimate column grey.\n\nKey insight\n- The bars encode arithmetic by height, with color providing the sign.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"963c33f8\" lines=\"86-108\">\n<description>\nDrop the columns of the top marker downward, stopping blue-ended columns on grey obstacles and letting maroon-ended columns pass to the bottom.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The most numerous non-background color forms stationary grey obstacle pixels.\n- The other two colors form a single 3x3 top-edge marker whose top two rows\n  are maroon and whose bottom row contains maroon or blue control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Erase the top marker.\n- Treat each marker column as a vertical drop retaining its three colors.\n- A column whose bottom control pixel is blue stops immediately above the\n  first grey pixel below that column, or bottom-aligns if none exists.\n- Contiguous maroon-ended columns pass through obstacles and bottom-align;\n  if such a run has a clear down-right slide from a grey ledge, shift the\n  run one column right before bottom-aligning.\n- When a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear\n  the rest of the marker-width gap in that bottom bar.\n\nKey insight\n- The bottom row of the top marker is a control strip: blue cells mean\n  \"stick to the first obstacle,\" while maroon cells mean \"fall through.\"\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"80a900e0\" lines=\"69-82\">\n<description>\nExtend diagonal rays outward from outer-border segments adjacent to diamond shapes on a checkerboard.\n\nInput structure:\n- Checkerboard bg (two most common colors).\n- Hollow diamond outlines (8-pixel) in a non-bg color (inner border).\n- Short diagonal line segments in another non-bg color adjacent to each diamond edge (outer border).\n\nRule:\n- For each outer-border segment, determine the outward direction (perpendicular to the segment, away from the diamond).\n- From each endpoint of the segment, cast a diagonal ray outward, painting over bg pixels with the segment's color until hitting a non-bg pixel or the grid edge.\n\nKey insight: Rays emit only from the two endpoints of each outer-border segment, not every pixel, and travel diagonally away from the diamond.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e8686506\" lines=\"172-185\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.\n- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.\n\nRule:\n- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.\n- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.\n- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.\n\nKey insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a644e277\" lines=\"31-50\">\n<description>\nExtract the lattice subregion bounded by the four background-colored corner holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A regular one-cell-thick lattice has one line color, one background color,\n  and sparse marker pixels.\n- Exactly four lattice-line intersections are background-colored holes instead\n  of line-color pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Find the unique non-background color that forms regular horizontal and\n  vertical lattice lines.\n- Use the four background-colored lattice intersections as the corners of one\n  rectangle.\n- Return the inclusive crop between those corner rows and columns.\n\nKey insight:\n- The crop is marked by the missing lattice intersections, not by the marker\n  pixels inside the cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"230f2e48\" lines=\"19-38\">\n<description>\nFold each straight red rod at its black marker, leaving the gray-anchored side fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each foreground object is a straight horizontal or vertical 4-connected rod.\n- Each rod contains many pixels of one line color, one endpoint marker, and one\n  interior control marker.\n\nRule:\n- For every rod, keep the segment from the endpoint marker through the control\n  marker unchanged.\n- Move the line-color tail on the far side of the control marker to a\n  perpendicular ray starting next to that marker.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
